# CASP15 T1188 — The Chitin Destroyer (8C6Z)
---

Necrotic enteritis kills millions of chickens
every year and costs the poultry industry
billions of dollars.

This is a chitinase from Clostridium perfringens —
one of the key weapons this pathogen uses
to attack.

Published in PLoS Pathogens in 2024.

> close
> load 8c6z
---

Chitinases break down chitin — the tough
structural material found in insect exoskeletons,
fungal cell walls, and crustacean shells.

But why does a gut bacterium need
a chitin-destroying enzyme?

> spin 0.15
---

The answer is that the mucus layer protecting
the chicken's gut contains chitin-like structures.

This chitinase helps Clostridium perfringens
chew through that protective barrier,
giving it access to the gut lining
where it causes devastating tissue damage.

> stop
> view ligand
---

Understanding these chitinases is important
because the poultry industry is under pressure
to stop using antibiotics in animal feed.

New treatments that target specific virulence
factors like this enzyme could replace
broad-spectrum antibiotics.

